{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Int.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Prod.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Logic.Embedding.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.kernel_summand_transpose_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.kernel_summand_transpose_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L118-L125","doc":"**The `cov(k)` summand is transpose-invariant.** Given a transpose-invariant weight `g` (the SYT\ndimension `f^λ = f^{λ'}`, classical) and `csq` transpose-even, the whole summand `g · (csq − C)` is\nfixed by transpose. Hence the transpose involution contributes **zero** information about the sign of\n`cov(k)` — the route-(b) no-go. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csum_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csum_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L108-L116","doc":"**Linear content is transpose-ODD.** `(j − i) + (i − j) = 0`, so `Σ_□ c(□)` negates under\ntranspose. This is the mechanism behind `T₂ = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csq_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csq_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L98-L106","doc":"**Content square is transpose-EVEN.** `(j − i)² = (i − j)²`, so `Σ_□ c(□)²` is fixed by\ntranspose. This is the fact that makes the transpose involution a *symmetry* of the `cov` summand —\nit cannot sign `T₃`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csq","kind":"def","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L94-L96","doc":"Content square `Σ_□ (j − i)²` of a diagram (in `ℤ`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.csum","kind":"def","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L90-L92","doc":"Linear content `Σ_□ (j − i)` of a diagram (in `ℤ`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.transpose","kind":"def","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L86-L88","doc":"Transpose of a diagram: swap the coordinates of every cell. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.odd_summand_involution_cancels","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.odd_summand_involution_cancels","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L71-L78","doc":"**Odd summands cancel in pairs.** If the weight is invariant (`g (σ i) = g i`) and the quantity\nis odd (`a (σ i) = − a i`), the paired summands `g i · a i` and `g (σ i) · a (σ i)` cancel. Summing\nthis pairing over the transpose involution is exactly why the odd content moment vanishes:\n`T₂ = Σ_λ (f^λ)³ Σ_□ c(□) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.even_summand_involution_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.YoungDiagramTranspose.even_summand_involution_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/YoungDiagramTranspose.lean#L63-L69","doc":"**Even moment is fixed pointwise.** If the weight `g` and the quantity `a` are both invariant\nunder `σ` at `i`, the weighted summand `g · a` is fixed by `σ`. So an involution gives **no** sign\ninformation about an even moment — the crux of the transpose no-go for `T₃`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}